---

<Base
  title="Why more good bets stop helping"
  description="Stacking strategies has a ceiling, the ceiling arrives early, and how you pay for each new one decides whether your edges add up or average out."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <Rail>The question</Rail>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">Why more good bets stop helping</h1>

    <MarginNote label="The question">
      <p>
        If I hold a lot of strategies that each beat the market 55 times out of a hundred, am I not close to
        certain to beat it? Checked against <code>docs/research/</code> on <span data-numeric>2026-08-23</span>.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Say you find a strategy that beats a cheap index fund 55 times out of a hundred. On its own that is nearly
    nothing. So find another one, and another, and keep going.</p>

    <p>Each is a slightly loaded coin. Flip enough loaded coins and the average has to come out ahead, and the
    more of them you flip the tighter the result gets. Twelve looks safe. A hundred looks certain.</p>

    <p>That is a good instinct, and it is not folk wisdom. It is a theorem. Richard Grinold wrote it down in 1989
    and quantitative firms have been built on it since: how good your record gets rises with the square root of
    the number of independent bets you place. Every word of that sentence is doing work, and one word is doing all
    of it.</p>

    <p>Independent.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-6 gap-y-2">
    <Rung level="settled" showGloss />
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The mechanism</Rail>

    <h2>Averaging cannot touch the part they share</h2>

    <p>Your strategies are not separate coins. Cheap US stocks and cheap Japanese stocks are one idea in two
    postcodes, and they had their bad decade at the same time. A fund that buys stocks which have been going up,
    and a managed-futures fund that buys bonds and currencies which have been going up, are the same instruction
    pointed at different markets.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Source">
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/FlanaganSe/kelly-portfolios/blob/main/docs/research/stacking-and-effective-breadth.md"
          >Stacking and effective breadth</a
        >, 422 months to <span data-numeric>2025-12</span>. Measured on modelled returns rather than on the funds'
        own: each piece is built from the exposure that kind of fund really delivers, applied to its own region's
        academic factor series.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>We measured this on the active parts of one real portfolio: three funds buying cheap stocks, one buying
    recent winners, and a trend fund, month by month over 422 months to December 2025. The average pair moves
    together at <Figure id="sleeve-correlation" inline />.</p>

    <p>That number is a measurement of that portfolio and of nothing else. It is not a constant of investing, and
    every figure on this page that depends on it inherits the same scope. Four cheap-and-rising stock funds plus a
    trend fund are close relatives. A genuinely wider stack, one holding stock styles and trend and credit and
    catastrophe risk, would come in lower and reach higher. What is general here is the shape, not the level: the
    ceiling is always <code>Φ(z₁/√ρ)</code>, and ρ is the whole game.</p>

    <p>Here is why that single number decides everything, and it is worth a minute, because the rest of the page
    is a consequence of it.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Why it flattens">
      <p>
        The shared part of a stack's noise cannot be averaged away, because it is present in every term. So the
        effective count of separate bets converges on 1 divided by the correlation. At 0.435 that is 2.3.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>When you average several things, the part they do not share gets quieter fast. It falls away roughly in
    proportion to how many you have, which is the coin-flip intuition you already trust. The part they do share
    does not get quieter at all. You cannot average away something that is present in every term. So the noise in
    your stack falls, then flattens, then sits on a floor made of whatever the strategies have in common. That
    floor is the correlation. Which is why the number of genuinely separate bets you own converges on 1 divided by
    the correlation, and then stops.</p>

    <p>At the 0.435 measured in that portfolio, the limit is 2.3 bets. Not eventually. Almost immediately. Halve
    the correlation and the limit doubles, which is why the honest instruction is to lower ρ rather than to raise
    the count.</p>
  </div>

  <Breakout>
    <StackingCeiling />
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose mt-4">
    <MarginNote label="Read the whole grid">
      <p>
        The row you are on is a fact about your own holdings rather than about investing. At 0.2 the ceiling is
        61.1%; at 0.1 it is 65.4%. Open the table under the chart for every cell.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>The top row is your mental model, and your mental model is correct. With truly unrelated bets a hundred of
    them reach <Figure id="independent-hundred" inline showUnit={false} />, which is a portfolio worth having.
    Read the row underneath it. A fifth of a point of shared behaviour costs you the entire top half of the
    table.</p>

    <p>At the 0.435 measured in this particular portfolio, an unlimited stack of 55% strategies reaches
    <Figure id="stacking-ceiling" inline showUnit={false} /> and stops. The portfolio's eight tickers turn out to
    be five active positions worth <Figure id="effective-bets" inline />, because three of the eight hold no
    active position at all. They are the benchmark.</p>

    <p>How many truly unrelated 55% bets would it take to be 90% sure? <Figure id="bets-for-ninety" inline />.
    That is a market maker's order book, not a brokerage account.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Two omissions</Rail>

    <h2>A win rate leaves out two things</h2>

    <p>Both of them can flip the sign.</p>

    <MarginNote label="The same sentence, twice">
      <p>
        Both readings invert <code>P = Φ(IR √T)</code> on the repository's own identity. Every stacking figure on
        this page uses the one-year reading, which is the most flattering one available.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>The first is a horizon. Over one year, beating the index 55% of the time is a genuinely good strategy. Over
    thirty years, the identical English sentence describes something roughly five times weaker, because you had
    three decades of evidence and the coin still only came up 55. Measured as edge per unit of drift, the one-year
    version is <Figure id="strength-at-one-year" inline /> and the thirty-year version is
    <Figure id="strength-at-thirty-years" inline />. Same words. Same number. Nothing else in common.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Source">
      <p>
        Read from the funds' own published rates and 19a-1 notices,
        <a href="https://github.com/FlanaganSe/kelly-portfolios/blob/main/docs/research/alternative-sleeves-audit.md"
          >our audit of the alternatives</a
        >, <span data-numeric>2026-08-23</span>. A distribution rate is not a return.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>The second is size. A win rate says nothing about how much you win. Win 55 times by a point and lose 45
    times by two points, and you have lost money while winning most of the time. Every product sold on a high win
    rate has that shape, because the shape is what produces the win rate. The crypto covered-call funds are the
    current example: headline distribution rates of
    <Figure id="covered-call-distribution-rates" inline showUnit={false} /> a year against 30-day SEC yields of
    <Figure id="covered-call-sec-yields" inline showUnit={false} />, and one fund's own filed notice estimates the
    whole payout as your own money handed back to you.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The crux</Rail>

    <h2>The benefit grows slowly and the cost grows in a straight line</h2>

    <p>Now the crux, and it is arithmetic rather than opinion.</p>

    <p>The benefit of adding a strategy grows with the square root of your effective breadth, and your effective
    breadth is capped, as above. The cost of adding a strategy grows in a straight line, forever. Fee, turnover,
    spread, tax. Nothing about a fee gets cheaper because you already own eleven other funds.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Grinold, 1989">
      <p>
        The fundamental law says a record improves with the square root of the number of <em>independent</em>
        bets. Everything on this page is a consequence of that one word.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>You also buy your best idea first. Nobody starts with their fourth-favourite. So every strategy after the
    first drags your average edge down at exactly the point where the breadth benefit is flattening out.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Stated, not measured">
      <p>
        The twelve candidate edges are a stated ladder rather than twelve funds we priced. The optimisation is
        exact and reproducible under a frozen specification; the inputs are assumptions, so the answer is a
        property of that ladder and not of markets.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>So put twelve strategies on a ladder to see what that trade-off does. Ranked best to worst, gross edges
    running from 3.0% a year down to nothing, each costing 40 basis points, each drifting 6% a year, all as alike
    as the ones measured above. Those twelve edges are a stated example rather than twelve funds we measured — the
    point is the shape, and the shape holds for any shelf whose candidates differ in quality. What is not invented
    is the optimisation: it is solved exactly, under a frozen specification, and you can run it yourself.</p>

    <p>The best equal-weighted portfolio holds <Figure id="ladder-equal-weight-optimum" inline />. Holding all
    twelve is about two thirds worse than holding only the best one. The same shape appears with the costs set to
    zero, because dilution alone does it; costs only sharpen it.</p>

    <p>The obvious objection is that nobody has to hold them equally. Weight them properly, with no restrictions,
    and adding strategies really does keep helping. So look at what the optimiser does when you hand it twelve
    correlated strategies. It goes long the best five and sells the other seven short. Almost all of the extra
    value lives in the short leg.</p>

    <p>Then put back the one restriction you actually have. You buy funds in a brokerage account and you cannot
    sell anything short. Run it again.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Scope">
      <p>
        Solved by enumerating all 4,095 supports, so the count carries no convergence tolerance. It falls to two
        at a correlation of 0.50 and rises to five at 0.10, and it depends on the candidates having different
        edges.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>The best long-only portfolio holds <Figure id="ladder-long-only-optimum" inline /> and never improves past
    three, however many candidates you offer it. Nine of the twelve sit at zero. That is the honest answer to
    "stack a ton": most of the benefit of breadth lives in positions a retail investor is not permitted to
    take.</p>

    <p>Two things move that count and both are worth knowing. Lower the correlation to 0.10 and the optimiser
    holds five; raise it to 0.70 and it holds two. And give all twelve candidates the <em>same</em> edge and it
    holds all twelve, at no loss at all — which is the version of the idea you were arguing for, and the version
    no real shelf offers. It is the spread in quality that lets an optimiser throw nine of them away. The ceiling
    of 57.6% is unchanged either way, so the two cases are different routes to one wall rather than an escape from
    it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The funding rule</Rail>

    <h2>How you pay for it decides whether the edges add or average</h2>

    <p>This is the most useful thing on this page, and almost everybody conflates the two cases.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Why the two differ">
      <p>
        An average can never exceed its largest member, and a sum has no ceiling. The expression for the gap
        contains no property of the strategy being added.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p><strong>Sell to buy.</strong> You have $100. You sell $20 of stocks and buy $20 of a trend fund. Your
    weights still add to 100. Your portfolio's edge is now the weighted average of the edges of its parts, and an
    average can never be larger than its largest member. You cannot stack your way past your own best idea. Every
    strategy you add pulls the average toward the newcomer, which is why adding your fifth-best idea makes your
    portfolio more like your fifth-best idea.</p>

    <p><strong>Finance it.</strong> You have $100 and you buy a fund that holds $100 of stocks and $50 of managed
    futures inside one share, using borrowing you never see. Your money is still $100. Your exposure is $150. Now
    the edges add rather than average, and a sum has no ceiling. Here "stack a ton" is literally the right
    instruction.</p>

    <p>Same four leaning stock funds, three ways of paying for them, priced on the portfolio in question under the
    most generous assumption about what the leaning earns:</p>
  </div>

  <Breakout scroll class="my-8">
    <table role="table" class="stack-table w-full border-collapse text-sm sm:min-w-[34rem]">
      <caption class="sr-only">Portfolio edge under three ways of paying for the same four funds</caption>
      <thead role="rowgroup">
        <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left">
          {
            FUNDING_COLUMNS.map((heading, i) => (
              <th role="columnheader" scope="col" class:list={["eyebrow py-2 pr-4", i === 1 && "text-right"]}>
                {heading}
              </th>
            ))
          }
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody role="rowgroup" class="text-ink-muted">
        {
          FUNDING_ROUTES.map((route) => (
            <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule last:border-0">
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-normal">
                {route.label}
              </th>
              <td role="cell" data-label={FUNDING_COLUMNS[1]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">
                <Figure id={route.figure} inline showUnit={false} />
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose mt-8">
    <MarginNote label="Scope">
      <p>
        All three rows are priced against a cheap index fund holding the same mix, under the assumptions most
        favourable to the portfolio, with 35% of your money available to move. Compared with each other they are
        informative. Compared with anything else on the site they are not.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>The portfolio in question is about 93% the first kind.</p>

    <MarginNote label="The test">
      <p>
        Add up your target weights. If they come to 100, every new fund competes with the funds you already hold,
        and the question is never whether a candidate is good.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Here is the test you can run on your own account tonight. Add up your target weights. If they come to 100,
    then every new fund is competing with the funds you already hold, and the right question about a candidate is
    never "is this good?" It is "is this better than whatever I would sell to buy it?" Most people ask the first
    question and then quietly answer the second one by selling whatever has done worst lately.</p>

    <p>If instead you hold a fund that delivers two exposures per dollar, the second exposure is genuinely added
    rather than swapped, and the only question is whether it covers its own financing.</p>

    <p>The gap between those two ways of paying is worth about <Figure id="funding-gap" inline showUnit={false} />
    a year on an all-stock starting portfolio. It contains nothing whatever about the strategy being added. It is
    larger than almost any extra return this project has managed to measure, which is a slightly embarrassing
    thing to discover after a year spent measuring them.</p>
  </div>

  <Callout kind="caveat" label="Three warnings, or this becomes a pitch for borrowing">
    <p>
      Financing costs money, and the fee table will not price it. One fund on the shelf files a 0.10% net expense
      ratio while holding 95.17% of its assets in a swap on an affiliated fund that charges 0.75%, which the fee
      table does not reach. All-in it costs about 0.81% a year today and about 0.99% when its waiver lapses in
      December 2026, against a plainly-labelled competitor at 0.99%. Two fact sheets report that near-tie as a
      ten-to-one gap.
    </p>
    <p>
      Size it by what you can hold through a bad decade rather than by what the arithmetic prefers, because the
      growth arithmetic never returns an interior answer. It rates 30% of exposure better than 10%, and 50% better
      than 30%, and it keeps going past the tested range and past the drawdown that would end you.
    </p>
    <p>
      And watch the concentration you are buying. In the portfolio measured here,
      <Figure id="active-variance-concentration" inline showUnit={false} /> of the active risk sits inside one
      fund from a boutique issuer, in a category where
      <Figure id="managed-futures-attrition" inline showUnit={false} /> of the funds filing at the start of our
      window had stopped filing within six and a half years.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The objection</Rail>

    <h2>Where the instinct is right, and what happened when we checked</h2>

    <p>You may already have the objection, and it is the best part of the original idea: a fund can look bad alone
    and still help, and a fund can look good alone and add nothing. Both are true. Both have exact formulas.</p>

    <p>What matters about a candidate is not its own return. It is the part of its return that your existing
    portfolio does not already provide, divided by the part of its risk your existing portfolio does not already
    carry. A fund that loses money by itself improves the whole if it loses at the right moments, which is all a
    hedge has ever been. A fund with a real edge adds nothing if you already own that edge under another name.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Order of the five">
      <p>
        What the holding is worth inside the portfolio, less what it is worth on its own. Exactly zero when a
        holding is unrelated to everything else held. In order: cheap US stocks, cheap developed foreign stocks,
        the international momentum fund, cheap emerging-market stocks, and trend.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Then we measured it on the actual portfolio, and it is barely there. For the five active positions, the
    difference between judging a holding alone and judging it inside the portfolio reads
    <Figure id="conditioning-terms" inline showUnit={false} />. Four of the five are negative. Nothing in this
    portfolio is rescued by the company it keeps, and the largest single effect is a penalty: the international
    momentum fund is <Figure id="idmo-trend-correlation" inline /> correlated with the managed-futures fund,
    because a trend fund is momentum wearing a different coat.</p>

    <p>The mechanism is real. Check it every time. On this portfolio it is not what is going wrong, and what is
    going wrong is duller: the edges are small on their own, they rest on rewards nobody can show are still
    positive, and stacking repairs neither problem.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The record</Rail>

    <h2>What it has looked like with real money</h2>

    <MarginNote label="Issuer evidence">
      <p>
        The issuer's own standardised NAV returns beside the benchmark the issuer itself prints, read
        <span data-numeric>2026-08-23</span> as of <span data-numeric>2026-07-31</span>. The window is 2.9 years
        and the benchmark holds one asset and borrows nothing, so it answers whether the second leg
        covered its own cost.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>The Return Stacked funds are this exact trade, sold retail. For five of them the benchmark the issuer
    prints on its own website is the base leg of the stack, which makes it an unusually clean read on whether the
    second dollar paid for itself. On the issuer's own standardised numbers to 31 July 2026, six of the seven
    scoreable funds trailed. The stocks-plus-managed-futures fund trailed the S&amp;P 500 by
    <Figure id="rsst-stack-drag" inline showUnit={false} /> a year since it launched, and the bond version trailed
    US aggregate bonds by <Figure id="rsbt-stack-drag" inline showUnit={false} /> a year.</p>

    <p>Then trend fired. Over the twelve months to July 2026 that same stocks-plus-trend fund returned
    <Figure id="rsst-trailing-year" inline showUnit={false} /> against the S&amp;P 500's
    <Figure id="rsst-trailing-year-benchmark" inline showUnit={false} />.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Exposure, not return">
      <p>
        <span data-numeric>+0.681</span>, 95% range <span data-numeric>+0.406 to +0.955</span>, on 31 filed
        monthly returns to <span data-numeric>2026-04</span>. That is exposure delivered, never return earned.
        Nothing here says the reward is still on the table.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Both of those are one fact, not two. The engineering works. We measured the fund's delivered trend exposure
    out of its own SEC filings at <Figure id="rsst-trend-loading" inline showUnit={false} />, with a sister fund
    holding no trend at all as a control, so it really does hold what it claims to hold. The patience is the
    product, and the patience is priced in years.</p>

    <MarginNote label="A forecast, not a record">
      <p>
        Asness's table is an explicitly hypothetical exercise on stated capital-market assumptions, with no sample
        period, no named index and no fund. Read it beside the Return Stacked rows above, which are a record and
        disagree.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>How big is the prize when it does work? About seventy basis points a year, and that is not our figure.
    Cliff Asness published it in March 2025, in the paper where he endorses this idea by name: a 60/40 portfolio
    compounds at <Figure id="asness-6040" inline showUnit={false} />; add a quarter in alternatives and sell
    stocks and bonds to fund them and it compounds at <Figure id="asness-funded" inline showUnit={false} />, no
    better than the 60/40 it came from; add the same alternatives financed and it compounds at
    <Figure id="asness-financed" inline showUnit={false} />. That middle number is this page's averaging point,
    arrived at independently, by the idea's most committed advocate.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>What is reachable</Rail>

    <h2>What is actually on the table</h2>

    <p>Count return engines, not tickers. An engine is a distinct economic mechanism with its own way of failing,
    and two funds that lose money in the same crisis are one engine.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Scope">
      <p>
        The lower end of the geography figure is momentum across three regions, the upper end value. All sixteen
        factor-and-region combinations plus trend are worth 10.23 of 16. An international allocation may be right
        for currency or valuation reasons, and should not be defended as diversification.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Spreading a single strategy across the US, developed markets and emerging markets buys about half an extra
    bet: <Figure id="geography-breadth" inline showUnit={false} /> of three. Adding a second style inside one
    region buys a whole extra bet and change, <Figure id="style-breadth" inline showUnit={false} /> of five,
    because cheap stocks and rising stocks are frequently opposites. Geography is nearly free breadth. Style is
    the real thing.</p>

    <p>On our count an ordinary investor can reach <Figure id="engines-reachable" inline showUnit={false} />:
    broad stocks, leaning toward one kind of stock, government bonds, corporate credit with the interest-rate risk
    stripped out, managed futures, catastrophe risk. Gold,
    commodities, crypto, tail hedges, buffered funds, volatility selling, merger arbitrage and market-neutral
    funds were each tested and set aside, either because they duplicate something you already own or because the
    retail container costs more than the mechanism pays.</p>

    <MarginNote label="One rule, twice">
      <p>
        Both of these come out of <code>T = (z·s/e)²</code> at 90% confidence.
        <a href="/how-sure/">How sure we are</a> separates that question from the different one of when a study
        could separate the same edge from zero.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Which brings the last number, and it is the one that should reframe the whole exercise. This portfolio
    drifts from a comparable cheap index fund by about <Figure id="tracking-error" inline showUnit={false} /> a
    year. At that much drift, thirty years of holding it can only demonstrate an edge of about
    <Figure id="thirty-year-floor" inline showUnit={false} /> a year, against a central estimate of
    <Figure id="central-edge" inline showUnit={false} />.</p>

    <p>It is built so that a lifetime of owning it cannot tell you whether it worked.</p>

    <p>Which is not an argument for doing nothing. It is an argument that your own account will never be your
    evidence, so each decision has to be justified before you make it rather than after. Pay less. Defer tax. Put
    each fund in whichever account taxes it least. Save more. Buy nothing you could not hold through a decade in
    which it does nothing at all.</p>

    <p>Stacking works. It runs out at about four good ideas.</p>
  </div>

  <Callout kind="change-your-mind">
    <p>
      A correlation between holdings measured under 0.2 on a wider stack, or a fund you can actually buy that
      borrows to add a genuinely different engine at a cost the fee table can see, would raise the ceiling on this
      page. Nothing about the arithmetic changes. The input does.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Base>
